{"query": "repo:Effect-TS/effect scheduler", "totalCount": 104, "results": [{"title": "Add portable Redis and PostgreSQL clients to Effect core", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8638", "state": "open", "createdAt": "2026-09-30T20:13:10Z", "updatedAt": "2026-10-03T00:58:51Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "Add native Redis and PostgreSQL clients under `effect/redis` and `effect/postgres`, built on generic platform socket and cryptography services. The core clients contain no Node imports or external driver dependencies. `@effect/redis` is absorbed into Effect, and `@effect/sql-pg` moves to `effect/postgres`.

Redis supports standalone, Cluster, Sentinel, RESP2/3, binary commands, typed command descriptors, pipelines, reserved sessions, transactions with WATCH, and channel, pattern, and sharded Pub/Sub. Pipelines batch writes per destination and return results per command. Confirmed successes and commands with uncertain outcomes are never replayed automatically. Subscriptions reconnect and re-subscribe. Persistence supports all three topologies, using slot-affine keys in Cluster.

PostgreSQL retains its native protocol codec, prepared statements, pooling, multiplexed queries, transactions, streaming, LISTEN/NOTIFY, cancellation, and SQL persistence integrations. Authentication uses portable Crypto primitives for MD5 and SCRAM. Small protocol frames share disjoint, write-once backing buffers; consecutive prepared-cache hits avoid redundant Map churn while preserving cache trimming and eviction. Pipelines collect frames directly and emit contiguous batches.

`SocketConnector` provides scoped TCP/TLS/Unix connections and TLS upgrades. Receive callbacks apply backpressure when they return an Effect. Closing an upgraded session releases its TLS wrapper and handles pending write errors through physical close. Node, Bun, and Deno provide connector layers; Bun and Deno use their Node-compatible networking APIs. `NodeRedis`, `BunRedis`, and `DenoRedis` remain convenience compositions of the core client, persistence service, sockets, and Crypto.

## Migration

- Import database modules from `effect/redis` and `effect/postgres`. Provide `SocketConnector` to Redis; PostgreSQL and Redis persistence also require `Crypto`. PostgreSQL custom `stream` factories become `connector` functions.
- Replace Redis adapters' raw `client` and Promise-based `use` with Effect operations. RESP2 is the default, and layer acquisition validates the connection. Deno's `hostname`/`db` become `socket.host`/`database`; Bun's `connectionTimeout` becomes `connectTimeout`. Configure Bun's URL explicitly or through `layerConfig`. Driver-specific TLS options belong on the platform connector.
- Standalone and Sentinel preserve persistence key layouts. Moving existing data into Cluster requires migrating to tagged keys. Custom persistence services must implement `scan` and `cluster`; `Redis.make` requires `scan` and `scriptHash`.
- Custom Crypto implementations must support MD5, HMAC, and PBKDF2. PostgreSQL authentication helpers now return Effects using Crypto. Custom socket readers must provide `run(onChunk)`; `Socket.makeReader` derives it from `pull`. Pulls, receive loops, and TLS upgrades have exclusive ownership.

See the [Redis README](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/630f587fb1b4f46cdc0f354731c1e25c8bef9397/packages/effect/src/redis/README.md), [PostgreSQL README](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/630f587fb1b4f46cdc0f354731c1e25c8bef9397/packages/effect/src/postgres/README.md), and [migration guide](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/630f587fb1b4f46cdc0f354731c1e25c8bef9397/migration/v3-to-v4.md).

## Validation

- Latest focused runs passed on Node, Bun 1.4, and Deno: 86 PostgreSQL connection unit tests and 28 native connection integration tests per runtime, including verified TLS with large Unicode/text and bytea pipelines. The 28 connector tests per runtime cover TCP/TLS, sliced byte views, FIFO/backpressure, closure with buffered writes, active error propagation, and listener cleanup; 34 NodeSocket tests also passed on Node.
