"}, {"title": "`Queue.take` can hang forever even though the queue has a message in it", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/issues/8471", "state": "closed", "createdAt": "2026-09-23T20:52:01Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-24T00:27:22Z", "isPullRequest": false, "body": "### What version of Effect is running?

`4.0.0-rc.116`, `4.0.0-rc.117`

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

`Queue.take` (and `takeN`, `takeAll`, `takeBetween`, `peek`) can hang forever even though the queue already holds a message. `take` first checks for a message and then registers the fiber as a waiting taker — two separate runtime operations. If the fiber's operation budget (`MaxOpsBeforeYield`) runs out between them, the fiber yields. Another fiber can `offer` in that gap; `offer` sees no registered taker so it schedules no wakeup. The taker then registers and waits for a wakeup that will only arrive on the *next* offer.

The gap is in `awaitTake` (`packages/effect/src/Queue.ts`). After `takers.add(resume)` there is no re-check of `self.messages` or `self.state.offers`:

```ts
export const take = <A, E>(self: Dequeue<A, E>): Effect<A, E> =>
  internalEffect.suspend(
    () => takeUnsafe(self) ?? internalEffect.andThen(awaitTake(self), take(self))
  )

const awaitTake = <A, E>(self: Dequeue<A, E>) =>
  internalEffect.callback<void, E>((resume) => {
    if (self.state._tag === "Done") return resume(self.state.exit)
    self.state.takers.add(resume) // no re-check of self.messages / self.state.offers
    // ...
  })
```

`takeBetween` (line 1411) and `peek` (line 1540) have the same shape. Affected code is at commit `34e09becb6b93b885bbdbc1da4780b7f848bd2d0`, lines 1454-1457, 2076-2082, and 671.

Reproduction (`node repro.ts`). `padding` moves the point where the taker's budget runs out:

```ts
import { Effect, Queue } from "effect"

const attempt = (padding: number) =>
  Effect.gen(function*() {
    const queue = yield* Queue.unbounded<number>()
    let pad: Effect.Effect<void> = Effect.void
    for (let i = 0; i < padding; i++) pad = Effect.andThen(pad, Effect.void)
    const taker = yield* Effect.forkDetach(Effect.andThen(pad, Queue.take(queue)))
    yield* Effect.forkDetach(Queue.offer(queue, 1))
    for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) yield* Effect.yieldNow
    return taker.pollUnsafe() === undefined && (yield* Queue.size(queue)) === 1
  })

const stuck: Array<number> = []
for (let p = 0; p <= 2112; p++) if (await Effect.runPromise(attempt(p))) stuck.push(p)
console.log(stuck) // rc.116 and rc.117: [1022]. Expected: []
```

Swapping `Queue.take` for `takeN(q, 1)`, `takeAll`, `takeBetween(q, 1, 5)` or `peek` also prints `[1022]`. With `MaxOpsBeforeYield` set to 8 via `Effect.provideService(Scheduler.MaxOpsBeforeYield, 8)`, the hang appears at padding 5. A single-taker, single-offer sweep over budgets 3-16 and paddings 0-40 left the taker waiting in 14 of 574 runs.

### What is the expected behavior?

`Queue.take`, `takeN`, `takeAll`, `takeBetween`, and `peek` on an open queue never stay suspended while the queue holds a message or a pending offer — regardless of `MaxOpsBeforeYield` value or where in the operation sequence the caller's budget runs out. The reproduction above should print `[]`.

### What do you see instead?

The taker fiber hangs indefinitely. The reproduction prints `[1022]` — padding value 1022 causes the taker to yield between the check and the registration, after which no wakeup is ever delivered. `Queue.size` still reports 1 (the message is there but never consumed).

### Additional information

**Affected:** `awaitTake` and its five callers in `packages/effect/src/Queue.ts`, plus `packages/effect/test/Queue.test.ts`.

**Unaffected:** `offer`, `offerUnsafe`, `offerAll`, and `releaseTakers` — these are correct once a taker is registered.

**A fix exists:** making `awaitTake` immediately resume when `self.messages.length > 0 || self.state.offers.size > 0` at registration time (so the caller loops back into `takeUnsafe`) makes every sweep print `[]`. Alternatively, doing the check and the registration in a single atomic operation closes the same gap.

**Non-goals / out of scope:**
- Fixing `take` alone and leaving `takeBetween`/`takeN`/`takeAll`/`peek` with the same gap
- A regression test that never forc"}, {"title": "Stop the Effect run loop building a success Exit between continuations", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8437", "state": "closed", "createdAt": "2026-09-23T10:37:31Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-23T23:27:21Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "`Effect.map`, `Effect.tap`, `Effect.exit`, `Effect.match` and `Effect.matchCause` used to wrap each step's result in a success `Exit` and return it to the run loop, which unwrapped it and passed the value to the next continuation. They now pass the value to the next continuation directly.

## How it works

`FiberImpl.succeedWith(value)` pops the next success continuation and calls it with the value, the same way `sync` and `withFiberSucceed` already do from `[evaluate]`.

A continuation that calls a continuation adds a JS frame, so an unbounded chain would overflow the stack on a deeply nested program. Every call to `succeedWith` increments `currentOpCount`, and every 32nd call returns an `Exit` to the run loop instead. That caps the nesting at 31 continuations. It also means each inlined step still counts as one operation against `MaxOpsBeforeYield`, the same as the `Exit` it replaces.

Interrupts are unchanged: `getCont` already returns the interrupt continuation when one is pending. Context-restoring frames (`onExit`) still return an `Exit`, so a tracer `context` hook sees the restored span before any continuation after the region runs.

A pending `shouldYield` check can run up to 31 operations late, because it's only checked in the run loop.

## Allocations per step

Measured with `v8.getHeapStatistics().total_allocated_bytes` over 1M-step chains on Node 26. Each figure includes the frame and stack growth that every step pays.

| step | main | this PR |
|---|---|---|
| `Effect.map` | 64.5 B | 33.7 B |
| two `Effect.map`s | 136.5 B | 74.8 B |
| `Effect.tap` | 113.6 B | 82.8 B |
| `Effect.exit` over a `map` | 136.7 B | 105.7 B |
| `Effect.match` / `Effect.matchCause` | 64.3 B | 33.3 B |
| `Effect.flatMap`, `Effect.as` | unchanged | unchanged |

On the same machine, a chain of ten steps also ran faster: `map` ~12%, `tap` ~11%, `match` ~20%. The `flatMap` control was unchanged.

## Tests

Each test fails if the invariant it names is broken, and all four also pass on main.

- `Effect.test.ts`, "stack safety": a 100,000-deep, lazily built chain of `map`s. Fails with `RangeError` if the inline bound is removed.
- `Effect.test.ts`, "interruption": a `map` callback that interrupts its own fiber skips the next `map`. Fails if a handoff ignores a pending interrupt.
- `Scheduler.test.ts`: two fibers running 100 `map`s each at `MaxOpsBeforeYield` 16 still interleave. Fails if inlined steps stop counting as operations.
- `Tracer.test.ts`, `Effect.withParentSpan`: a tracer `context` hook sees the restored span before a continuation after the region runs. Fails if a context-restoring frame hands off instead of returning to the run loop.

Closes EFF-1540



