"}, {"title": "Fix PubSub losing a message, or publishing one, when a waiter is interrupted", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8449", "state": "closed", "createdAt": "2026-09-23T10:53:24Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-23T22:40:21Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "A waiter could be interrupted between registering with PubSub and installing its cleanup. A stale subscriber poller then swallowed a later message; a stale backpressured publisher could publish after interruption.

Register each waiter inside `Effect.callback` and return the unregister action from that callback, so interruption cannot land between registration and cleanup installation. The callbacks use a private Deferred for completion and handle completion during registration before subscribing to its result. The subscriber callback also checks for shutdown after entering the callback.

The interruption regressions live in `packages/effect/test/PubSub.test.ts` alongside the existing PubSub tests. They fail on main at specific scheduler budgets and pass with the fix. The test suite also covers FIFO waiting takes and unsubscribe during a pending take.

Validation: PubSub tests (56 passed), effect TypeScript build, and dprint check.

Closes EFF-1551
"}, {"title": "Stop Stream.concat chains slowing down quadratically with their length", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8445", "state": "closed", "createdAt": "2026-09-23T10:47:12Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-23T22:20:25Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "**Merge after #8412.** This PR changes how many operations each pull takes. Without #8412, that makes the existing `Queue` lost wake-up hang `Stream.interruptWhen` in more configurations (351 → 1,069 of 26,784 in a sweep). With #8412 applied, none of them hang.

A chain of streams joined with `Stream.concat` is one sequence however it is nested, but each `concat` currently adds a layer that every element inside it passes through. Folding `concat` over pages, files or batches therefore gets quadratically slower as the chain grows.

## How

This follows how ZIO keeps `++` linear. A `concat` now runs as a single layer over a stack of pending parts. When the next part is itself a `concat`, its operands go on the stack instead of starting another layer.

- The operands are stored in a module-level `WeakMap` keyed by the `concat` result. The stream object itself is unchanged: it has the same keys and `Equal.equals` behaviour, and a copy with a different channel runs that channel.
- The stack is created for each run, so running a stream twice starts from the beginning each time.
- Each part still runs in its own scope, which closes before the next part starts.

## Results

Operations per part in a left-nested chain:

| n | main | this PR |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 100 | 146 | 13.2 |
| 1,000 | 1,046 | 13.0 |
| 10,000 | 10,046 | 13.0 |

Right-nested chains show the same pattern, and balanced chains go from O(n log n) to O(n). For a left-nested chain of 10,000, time per element drops from about 205 µs to about 490 ns, and allocations per part fall about 4–9×. A single two-part `concat` doesn't regress: it is slightly faster and allocates slightly less.

## Tests

- The work per part stays flat when a left- or right-nested chain doubles in length. The test counts scheduler operations, not wall-clock time.
- A nested chain runs in order and closes each part before the next starts, including when the same stream is run twice.
- A copy of a chain with a different channel runs its own channel.

**Not covered:** recursion through `Stream.suspend` (`concat(s, suspend(() => loop))`) is still quadratic, as on main.

Closes EFF-1547
"}, {"title": "Fix a permit leak in PartitionedSemaphore and make transactional waiters interruptible", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8391", "state": "closed", "createdAt": "2026-09-22T19:01:21Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-23T14:42:46Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "**Closed:** not split out yet.

Two ways of taking a permit go wrong when the taker is interrupted.
`PartitionedSemaphore` takes the permits one step before it installs the
release, so an interrupt in between keeps them. `TxSemaphore` and
`TxReentrantLock` take the opposite position and make the whole acquire
uninterruptible, including the wait, so a parked waiter cannot be cancelled at
all. Both are the same rule read twice: taking the resource and installing its
release are one step, and the wait before it is not part of that step.

Each bug has a test that fails on `main`. `Scheduler.MaxOpsBeforeYield` set to a
small value makes a fiber yield at every point between two steps, which turns a
window into a deterministic case; a budget below 3 never makes progress, so the
sweeps run 3..64.

## Bugs

| # | primitive | what went wrong | symptom | fails on main at | test |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `PartitionedSemaphore.withPermits` | acquires inside `restore`, installs the release after the wait returns | permits kept with no holder: `available` drops to 0 and every later `withPermits` waits forever | budget 7 | `an interrupted withPermits returns its permit at every yield` |
| 2 | `PartitionedSemaphore.withPermitsIfAvailable` | takes the permits in `suspend`, installs `ensuring` one step later | same leak, same deadlock | budget 4 | `an interrupted withPermitsIfAvailable returns its permit at every yield` |
| 3 | `TxSemaphore.withPermit`, `withPermits`, `withPermitScoped` | the acquiring transaction parks on `Effect.txRetry` inside the uninterruptible acquire of `acquireUseRelease` or `acquireRelease` | interrupt, `timeout` and `race` cannot cancel a waiter until a permit frees | deterministic; each test hangs until the test timeout on main | `interrupting a withPermit waiter completes, and leaks no permit`, `a timeout cancels a withPermits waiter`, `interrupting a withPermitScoped waiter completes` |
| 4 | `TxReentrantLock.withReadLock`, `withWriteLock`, `readLock`, `writeLock` | same shape | same: a waiting reader or writer cannot be cancelled | deterministic; hangs until the test timeout on main | `interrupting a withWriteLock waiter completes, and the lock stays usable`, `interrupting a readLock waiter completes` |

Bug 2 is not in the report this branch started from; it is the same shape one
function further down the file.

## Fixes

| # | fix | who owns the fact now |
|---|---|---|
| 1, 2 | one masked step takes what is available and registers a waiter for the rest, and installs the release in that same step; only the wait for the rest is interruptible | the call's waiter record: it holds `requested - owed` permits at every moment, granted or not, so one release settles either outcome |
| 3, 4 | the acquiring transaction never retries: it takes the resource or reports that it could not, and the release is installed in the same uninterruptible step. Waiting is a separate transaction that only reads and retries | the taking transaction, which is atomic by construction, and the scope or exit frame that holds the release |

The Tx fix is `txAcquireUseRelease` / `txAcquireScoped`, shared by both modules.
A waiter parked in the read-only transaction is an ordinary interruptible
`txRetry` park, which is what `TxQueue.take` already relies on.

**Behaviour this changes on purpose.** Splitting the transactional acquire into
"wait, then take" means a woken waiter can find the resource gone and park
again, so the order in which several `TxSemaphore` waiters woken by one release
are granted is not the same as on `main`. `TxSemaphore` documents no ordering
guarantee and `main`'s own order already varies with the op budget (a probe with
three waiters and one `releaseN` gives `a,b,c` on `main` at budgets `-` and 7
and `a,c,b` at 4, 9, 13 and 30; head gives `c,a,b`, `a,c,b` and `a,b,c` at the
same budgets), and permit accounting is exact on both sides in every case.
`PartitionedSemaphore`'s documented round-robin grant order across p"}]}
{"query": "repo:Effect-TS/effect "atom-react" "19.3"", "totalCount": 1, "results": [{"title": "Add an Effect-native TDS driver for sql-mssql", "url": "https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/pull/8129", "state": "open", "createdAt": "2026-09-08T13:04:21Z", "updatedAt": "2026-10-02T07:05:56Z", "isPullRequest": true, "body": "## Summary

Following the native PostgreSQL driver in #7426, replace `@effect/sql-mssql`'s
tedious runtime dependency with a native TDS 7.4 implementation owned by Effect.
Tedious remains a devDependency only for reproducible comparisons.

This is a **draft**, not a claim of complete tedious compatibility.

## Structure

The driver follows `@effect/sql-pg`'s module layout and coding style. Each module
depends only on the ones below it:

| Module | Role | sql-pg counterpart |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `MssqlClient` | `SqlClient` facade, typed parameters, stored procedures, `layer`/`layerConfig`/`layerFrom` | `PgClient` |
| `MssqlPool` | Pools of sessions, retiring sessions that fail fatally | `PgPool` |
| `MssqlConnection` | One session: TLS, login, routing, retries, requests, cancellation | `PgConnection` |
| `MssqlAuth` | Pure NTLMv2; callers supply the client challenge and time | `PgAuth` |
| `MssqlProtocol` | Pure TDS codec: packets, `PRELOGIN`/`LOGIN7`, requests, tokens, values, SQL Browser messages | `PgProtocol` |
| `MssqlTypes` | Parameter type descriptors | — |

As in `PgProtocol`, the codec uses a pooled `Writer` and a reusable `Reader` over
`Uint8Array`, closure-based incremental parsers, tagged `ParseError`/`EncodeError`,
`Result`-returning encoders, and named protocol constants. Server errors are
classified in `internal/sqlError.ts`, and tests are organized per module
(`*.test.ts`, `*.in-process.test.ts`, `*.integration.test.ts`).

Effect 4.0.0 marks the SQL Server parameter-type API `@stability unstable`; the
new `MssqlTypes`, `MssqlProtocol`, `MssqlAuth`, `MssqlConnection`, and `MssqlPool`
modules are marked unstable as well. `MssqlClient` and `MssqlMigrator` keep their
stable status.

Two deliberate differences from sql-pg: TDS rows are not length-prefixed, so column
value codecs live in `MssqlProtocol` next to the token grammar; and transaction
control is sent as SQL batches, so transactions opened by it belong to the session
rather than an `sp_executesql` scope.

## Implemented

- Incremental bounded packet/token parsing and native RPC/parameter codecs.
- TLS with certificate verification by default, LOGIN7 SQL authentication,
  NTLMv2 and Security Token FedAuth, named-instance SSRP discovery, routing and
  bounded startup retries. Access tokens are acquired through an Effect for each
  new pooled connection. LOGIN7 also advertises UTF-8 support.
- Scalar, Unicode/ANSI/collation, binary, date/time, decimal, LOB and TVP support;
  stored procedures, output parameters and return values.
- Effect-scoped connections, pooling, transactions/savepoints, serialized
  requests, timeouts and ATTENTION acknowledgement before reuse.
- Native public `MssqlTypes`, preserving SQL bigint strings and numeric results.
- Procedure calls respect the transaction's pinned connection.

## Validation

Rebased onto the Effect `4.0.0` release. Local results on `aa61eeb79`:

- `pnpm lint`, `pnpm check`, `pnpm circular`, and `pnpm jsdocs --check` pass;
  `pnpm codegen` produces no diff.
- 73 unit and in-process tests pass.
