across the two zones. Other hosted stages expose their own workers.dev address. Destroying
a Preview removes only its Website Preview and matching API.

Public content, canonical metadata and AI discovery use Common Practice and the
new website origin. Agent discovery lists `common-practice-website`; the former
`daw-website/SKILL.md` URL serves the same document for saved clients. Public MCP
identifies as `common-practice`, while its tool and prompt names stay stable.
Telemetry event names, runtime bindings, package names and stack identifiers stay
stable. The production trace filter includes both website origins to preserve
old traffic in the existing dashboards. These are source declarations; the
cutover checklist and dated evidence record deployment separately.

Native development runs Website port 3210 and API port 3211, both strict.
It needs the existing Doppler development credentials for Alchemy's state.
There is no single-app `dev:ui` fallback. The Website's native build hash includes
shared packages, content sources, API sources and the lockfile, so content-only
changes rebuild its version and refresh its cache.

The compatibility date is `2026-09-22`, with `nodejs_compat` and
`enable_request_signal`. Website bindings are explicitly `COMMON_PRACTICE_STAGE`, `BACKEND`,
`COMMON_PRACTICE_API_URL`, `COMMON_PRACTICE_WEBSITE_URL` and checked `COMMON_PRACTICE_POSTHOG_CONFIG`, plus scoped
Axiom values on hosted stages.
The API binds its checked stage and native self URL plus the same hosted
telemetry values. Preview never points at production's API. Deployment
credentials never become Worker bindings.

Shared zone, TLS, DNSSEC and crawler policy owners live in
`@common-practice/infrastructure`. The separate production-only `daw-observability` and
`daw-trace-export` stacks keep their existing names, shared Axiom outputs and
removal rules. This app split does not change their resources or dashboards.
Only native Alchemy may plan, apply or destroy. The
[deployment runbook](docs/runbooks/deployment.md) owns those procedures.

## Observability

`@common-practice/analytics` owns checked settings, capture events and the two
closed Effect services. The browser service is private to
`apps/web/src/lib/analytics/browser.adapter.layer.ts`, inside the existing Atom
runtime. It acquires the SDK automatically on enabled deployments, only at
the matching Website origin without Do Not Track. There is no consent UI,
stored choice or consent operation. The adapter removes the old Common Practice choice key
and clears any SDK opt-out left by the former controls without an opt-in event.
It disables automatic
capture, recordings, flags, surveys and person profiles. Its synchronous consumer
strips SDK metadata, full URLs, session IDs and person fields. Page and navigation
capture share an Effect semaphore, with consecutive duplicate page views skipped.
Scope closes the client on React unmount. Acquisition failure leaves the service
usable with capture off, preserving page loading and WebMCP.

The SDK sends its selected batches to the checked browser path `/ingest/e/`.
The Website's private `analytics/relay/live.layer.ts` forwards each attempt
once to fixed `https://us.i.posthog.com/e/` through native Effect HttpClient.
`runtime.server.ts` composes it in the existing runtime; the reserved TanStack
route uses that runtime's single runner. The boundary checks the deployment,
exact Website origin, path, method, query and media type. It preserves opaque
batch bytes, caps both bodies at 1 MiB and gives all body/delivery work five
seconds. Scope releases work on failure or cancellation. Redirects are refused;
cookies, caller trace fields, visitor IP headers and authorisation are excluded.
Checked upstream statuses are returned with `no-store`, without Website retries
or direct fallback. The browser keeps its normal batching and retries; loss is
still possible. This route is `analytics-relay` request telemetry, independent
of Content reads and API usage. Assets, remote configuration, flags and replay
have no relay routes. Backend capture keeps its direct host.

The relay's schemas, errors and contract have separate owners. `request.ingress.ts`
checks HTTP input, `response.egress.ts` maps the checked reply, and the shared
`body.ingress.ts` uses native `Stream.limitBytes`, failing when the limit is exceeded.
Optional query fields become `Option` values at ingress and are encoded only
when sending the upstream request.

The API decorates its one Content service to collect completed reads through
HTTP, RPC, MCP and publishing, including failed reads. A request holds at most
64 reads, adds one request-completed event, and schedules one batch through
native Alchemy `WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil`. Streaming responses complete
their batch when the body closes. Health and preflight requests are skipped.
The native HttpClient sender has a five-second timeout and no retry or queue.
Request IDs are independent of browser visitor IDs. There are no request bodies,
raw paths, full queries, headers or raw IP fields in the capture vocabulary.

`alchemy.posthog.run.ts` owns `daw-analytics` at stage `production`: two projects
(Common Practice production and Common Practice preview), four private dashboards, twelve insights and
twelve tiles. Its private Distilled
adapter performs management operations and checks every response. Exact markers
prevent adopting unrelated objects; all lookup pages are read before recovery.
Projects retain event history and refuse replacement. Removing child declarations
soft-deletes only their owned dashboard, insight or tile. App deployment references
import {
  CommonPracticeObservability,
  declareCommonPracticeAxiomResources,
} from "@common-practice/infrastructure/axiom/resources";
import { sharedDatasets } from "@common-practice/infrastructure/axiom/types";
import {
  requireProductionStage,
  telemetryStackProps,
} from "@common-practice/infrastructure/stacks/telemetry";
import * as Effect from "effect/Effect";

/** Common Practice owns its dashboards and ingest token; Site continues to own the datasets. */
export default CommonPracticeObservability.make(
  telemetryStackProps,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    yield* requireProductionStage;
    const { dashboardCount } = yield* declareCommonPracticeAxiomResources(sharedDatasets);
    return { dashboardCount };
  }),
);
import {
  CommonPracticeTraceExport,
  declareCommonPracticeTraceDestination,
} from "@common-practice/infrastructure/cloudflare/traces";
import {
  requireProductionStage,
  telemetryStackProps,
} from "@common-practice/infrastructure/stacks/telemetry";
import * as Effect from "effect/Effect";

/** Separate state keeps dashboard updates independent of Cloudflare trace permissions. */
export default CommonPracticeTraceExport.make(
  telemetryStackProps,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    yield* requireProductionStage;
    const destination = yield* declareCommonPracticeTraceDestination();
    // Hosted Websites read this persisted name. Never output the destination's headers.
    return { destinationName: destination.name };
  }),
);
import * as Schema from "effect/Schema";
import { DeploymentStage } from "../../schemas";
import { IsolateId, IsolateRequestSeq } from "../isolate-identity";
import { requestEventName, projectName } from "../../observability/names";

export const RequestSurface = Schema.Literals(["tanstack_page", "effect_http"]);
export type RequestSurface = typeof RequestSurface.Type;

export const RouteFamily = Schema.Literals([
  "page",
  "page_api",
  "rpc",
  "mcp",
  "discovery",
  "representation",
  "analytics-relay",
  "other",
]);
export type RouteFamily = typeof RouteFamily.Type;

export const RequestMethod = Schema.Literals(["GET", "HEAD", "POST", "OTHER"]);
export type RequestMethod = typeof RequestMethod.Type;

export const TrafficCategory = Schema.Literals([
  "Browser-like",
  "Reported crawler",
  "Reported script",
  "Other",
  "Unknown",
]);
export type TrafficCategory = typeof TrafficCategory.Type;

// The crawlers named in the log record, written once. A user-agent matches a name
// when it contains it, ignoring case.
export const namedAiBots = [
  "ChatGPT-User",
  "OAI-SearchBot",
  "GPTBot",
  "Claude-SearchBot",
  "Claude-User",
  "ClaudeBot",
  "anthropic-ai",
  "Perplexity-User",
  "PerplexityBot",
  "Googlebot",
  "Bingbot",
  "Bytespider",
  "Applebot",
  "meta-externalagent",
  "meta-externalfetcher",
] as const;

export const AiBot = Schema.Literals([...namedAiBots, "Other reported crawler", "None"]);
export type AiBot = typeof AiBot.Type;

const ResponseStatus = Schema.Int.check(Schema.isBetween({ minimum: 100, maximum: 599 }));

export const RequestObservationEvent = Schema.Struct({
  event: Schema.Literal(requestEventName),
  project: Schema.Literal(projectName),
  stage: DeploymentStage,
  surface: RequestSurface,
  routeFamily: RouteFamily,
  method: RequestMethod,
  outcome: Schema.Literals(["completed", "failed", "cancelled"]),
  statusGroup: Schema.Literals(["1xx", "2xx", "3xx", "4xx", "5xx", "unknown"]),
  status: Schema.optional(ResponseStatus),
  isolateId: Schema.optional(IsolateId),
  requestSeq: IsolateRequestSeq,
  trafficCategory: TrafficCategory,
  aiBot: AiBot,
  worker: Schema.optional(Schema.Literals(["website", "backend"])),
});
export type RequestObservationEvent = typeof RequestObservationEvent.Type;
import * as Boolean from "effect/Boolean";
import * as Cause from "effect/Cause";
import * as Effect from "effect/Effect";
import * as Exit from "effect/Exit";
import * as Option from "effect/Option";
import * as Schema from "effect/Schema";
import * as Headers from "effect/unstable/http/Headers";
import type * as HttpServerRequest from "effect/unstable/http/HttpServerRequest";
import type { DeploymentStage } from "../../schemas";
import type { IsolateRequest } from "../isolate-identity";
import { requestEventName, projectName } from "../../observability/names";
import { classifyMethod, classifyTraffic, classifyAiBot, statusGroup } from "./classification";
import { RequestObservationEvent } from "./schemas";
import type { RequestSurface, RouteFamily } from "./schemas";
import { RequestObservationError } from "./errors";

export interface RequestObservationInput {
  readonly request: HttpServerRequest.HttpServerRequest;
  readonly stage: DeploymentStage;
  readonly surface: RequestSurface;
  readonly routeFamily: RouteFamily;
  readonly isolate: IsolateRequest;
  readonly worker: Option.Option<"website" | "backend">;
}

// Build only fixed labels, then check the complete outgoing log record.
export const encodeRequestObservation = Effect.fnUntraced(function* <E>({
  input,
  exit,
}: {
  readonly input: RequestObservationInput;
  readonly exit: Exit.Exit<{ readonly status: number }, E>;
}) {
  const userAgent = Headers.get(input.request.headers, "user-agent");
  const status = Exit.match(exit, {
    onFailure: () => Option.none<number>(),
    onSuccess: (response) => Option.some(response.status),
  });
  const event: RequestObservationEvent = {
    event: requestEventName,
    project: projectName,
    stage: input.stage,
    surface: input.surface,
    routeFamily: input.routeFamily,
    method: classifyMethod(input.request.method),
    outcome: Exit.match(exit, {
      onFailure: (cause) =>
        Boolean.match(Cause.hasInterrupts(cause), {
          onFalse: () => "failed",
          onTrue: () => "cancelled",
        }),
      onSuccess: () => "completed",
    }),
    statusGroup: statusGroup(status),
    requestSeq: input.isolate.requestSeq,
    trafficCategory: classifyTraffic(userAgent),
    aiBot: classifyAiBot(userAgent),
    ...Option.match(input.worker, {
      onNone: () => ({}),
      onSome: (worker) => ({ worker }),
    }),
  };
  // Leave a key out when it has no value, so no log field is undefined.
  const withIsolate = Option.match(input.isolate.isolateId, {
    onNone: () => event,
    onSome: (isolateId) => ({ ...event, isolateId }),
  });
  const complete = Option.match(status, {
    onNone: () => withIsolate,
    onSome: (responseStatus) => ({ ...withIsolate, status: responseStatus }),
  });
  return yield* Schema.encodeEffect(RequestObservationEvent)(complete).pipe(
    Effect.mapError(() => new RequestObservationError({ reason: "invalid-event" })),
  );
});

